  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Pankaj Rao vs State Of U.P. And Another on 19 February, 2021 Author: Siddharth Bench: Siddharth",
    "facts": "The instant Anticipatory Bail Application has been filed with a prayer to grant an anticipatory bail to the applicant, namely, Pankaj Rao, in Case Crime No.- 09 of 2021, under Sections- 272 I.P.C and 60 and 60(2) of Excise Act, Police Station-Rampur Karhhana, District-Deoria. The applicant has been implicated for offences under Section 272 I.P.C., which is non-bailable offences. Along with the aforesaid section the applicant has also been implicated for offences under Sections 60 and 60(2) of Excise Act. It has been submitted that implication under Section 272 IPC is unjustified since there is a special Act holding the field, i.e. food safety and Standards Act 2006. False recovery has been made against the applicant. The police is regularly making efforts to arrest the applicant. The offence under sections 60 & 60 (2) of Excise Act is bailable and triable by Magistrate. Learned AGA has opposed the prayer for anticipatory bail of the applicant.",
    "arguments": "He has submitted that in view of the seriousness of the allegations made against the applicant, he is not entitled to grant of anticipatory bail. The apprehension of the applicant is not founded on any material on record. Only on the basis of imaginary fear anticipatory bail cannot be granted. In the case of Joginder Kumar v. State of Uttar Pradesh AIR 1994 SC 1349 the Apex Court has referred to the third report of National Police Commission wherein it is mentioned that arrests by the police in India is one of the chief source of corruption in the police. The report suggested that, by and large, nearly 60 percent of the arrests were either unnecessary or unjustified and that such unjustified police action accounted for 43.2 percent of expenditure of the jails. Personal liberty is a very precious fundamental rights and it should be curtailed only when it becomes imperative. According to the peculiar facts and circumstances of the peculiar case the arrest of an accused should be made. Without expressing any opinion on the merits of the case and considering the nature of accusation and his antecedents, the applicant is entitled to be released on anticipatory bail for limited period in this case considering the exceptions considered by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the case of Sushila Aggarwal vs. State (NCT of Delhi)- 2020 SCC Online SC 98.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "He has submitted that in view of the seriousness of the allegations made against the applicant, he is not entitled to grant of anticipatory bail. The apprehension of the applicant is not founded on any material on record. Only on the basis of imaginary fear anticipatory bail cannot be granted.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In the case of Joginder Kumar v. State of Uttar Pradesh AIR 1994 SC 1349 the Apex Court has referred to the third report of National Police Commission wherein it is mentioned that arrests by the police in India is one of the chief source of corruption in the police. The report suggested that, by and large, nearly 60 percent of the arrests were either unnecessary or unjustified and that such unjustified police action accounted for 43.2 percent of expenditure of the jails. Personal liberty is a very precious fundamental rights and it should be curtailed only when it becomes imperative. According to the peculiar facts and circumstances of the peculiar case the arrest of an accused should be made. Without expressing any opinion on the merits of the case and considering the nature of accusation and his antecedents, the applicant is entitled to be released on anticipatory bail for limited period in this case considering the exceptions considered by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the case of Sushila Aggarwal vs. State (NCT of Delhi)- 2020 SCC Online SC 98."
  },
